The problem is that the `for` loop that prints the array is inside the `while` loop that reads the file.  
Because the file is read line‑by‑line, the array is printed after every line, so you see the
array growing with each iteration.  
Move the printing code outside the `while` loop (or put it in a separate function) so that it
runs only once after the file has been fully read.

```php
<?php
$filename = 'file.txt';
$lines = array();

if (file_exists($filename)) {
    $handle = fopen($filename, 'r');
    if ($handle) {
        while (($line = fgets($handle)) !== false) {
            $lines[] = trim($line);
        }
        fclose($handle);
    }
}

// Print the array once, after the file has been read
foreach ($lines as $line) {
    echo $line . PHP_EOL;
}
?>
```

Now the array will be printed only once, after all lines have been read.
